1
00:00:00,800 --> 00:00:04,419
Some of this static is left
over from the Big Bang.

2
00:00:06,801 --> 00:00:10,116
If you grew up with an old
television, you've seen it.

3
00:00:10,116 --> 00:00:13,502
Between the channels: a
hissing, crawling snow.

4
00:00:15,098 --> 00:00:17,989
Most of it was made right here on Earth.

5
00:00:17,989 --> 00:00:23,405
Warm electronics inside the set.
Distant transmitters. Even the Sun.

6
00:00:24,738 --> 00:00:28,624
But a tiny part of it, often
quoted as about one percent,

7
00:00:28,624 --> 00:00:34,168
though it was never precisely measured,
came from much, much farther away.

8
00:00:35,764 --> 00:00:38,622
It is the oldest light in the universe.

9
00:00:42,314 --> 00:00:49,492
Go back nearly fourteen billion years. The young
universe is a hot, dense fog of charged particles.

10
00:00:49,492 --> 00:00:53,255
Light can't travel far
before it's scattered.

11
00:00:54,589 --> 00:00:59,240
Then, about three hundred and eighty
thousand years after the Big Bang,

12
00:00:59,240 --> 00:01:02,818
the fog cools to around
three thousand degrees.

13
00:01:03,889 --> 00:01:09,144
Electrons settle onto atoms. And for
the first time, light breaks free.

14
00:01:10,216 --> 00:01:14,256
The whole universe glows like
the surface of a cool star:

15
00:01:14,256 --> 00:01:17,591
a dull orange light, everywhere at once.

16
00:01:20,235 --> 00:01:22,894
That light has been travelling ever since.

17
00:01:22,894 --> 00:01:27,193
And as space expanded, it
stretched the light along with it.

18
00:01:28,265 --> 00:01:29,699
Orange became red.

19
00:01:29,699 --> 00:01:38,803
Red became infrared. The waves grew about a thousand
times longer, until the glow was invisible: microwaves.

20
00:01:40,137 --> 00:01:44,385
Today it is just two point seven
degrees above absolute zero.

21
00:01:44,385 --> 00:01:48,367
And it reaches us from
every direction in the sky.

22
00:01:49,700 --> 00:01:55,282
Roughly four hundred of these ancient
photons fill every cubic centimetre of space.

23
00:01:55,282 --> 00:01:58,270
Including the room you're sitting in.

24
00:01:59,866 --> 00:02:04,904
An old TV antenna, tuned to nothing,
catches a faint whisper of it.

25
00:02:07,548 --> 00:02:08,254
In 1964,

26
00:02:08,254 --> 00:02:17,574
at Bell Labs in New Jersey, Arno Penzias and Robert
Wilson were testing a giant horn-shaped antenna.

27
00:02:18,384 --> 00:02:23,515
It had been built to catch faint signals
from the first communication satellites.

28
00:02:23,515 --> 00:02:27,309
It was one of the quietest
radio receivers on Earth.

29
00:02:28,381 --> 00:02:31,329
They found a faint hiss
they couldn't get rid of.

30
00:02:31,329 --> 00:02:34,477
Day or night. Whichever way they pointed it.

31
00:02:35,549 --> 00:02:38,674
They even evicted a pair of nesting pigeons,

32
00:02:38,674 --> 00:02:44,358
and cleaned out what Penzias politely
called "white dielectric material".

33
00:02:45,691 --> 00:02:46,926
The hiss remained.

34
00:02:46,926 --> 00:02:55,177
Nearby, at Princeton, physicists had been preparing to
search for exactly this: the afterglow of a hot beginning.

35
00:02:56,249 --> 00:03:02,244
When their leader, Robert Dicke, heard about
the Bell Labs hiss, he reportedly told his team:

36
00:03:02,244 --> 00:03:04,485
"Boys, we've been scooped."

37
00:03:05,557 --> 00:03:11,450
In 1978, Penzias and Wilson
shared the Nobel Prize in Physics.

38
00:03:14,094 --> 00:03:14,884
Since then,

39
00:03:14,884 --> 00:03:21,129
the COBE, WMAP and Planck spacecraft have
mapped this glow across the whole sky.

40
00:03:22,201 --> 00:03:24,263
It is almost perfectly smooth.

41
00:03:24,263 --> 00:03:31,637
Its tiny ripples, about one part in a hundred
thousand, are the seeds of everything that came later.

42
00:03:32,708 --> 00:03:39,312
The warmer and cooler spots trace places that
were very slightly denser or thinner than average.

43
00:03:39,312 --> 00:03:44,353
Over billions of years, gravity
pulled the denser places together.

44
00:03:45,163 --> 00:03:47,831
Galaxies. Stars. Planets. Us.

45
00:03:50,737 --> 00:03:55,506
Today, analog television is
gone from most of the world.

46
00:03:55,506 --> 00:04:03,925
The United States switched off full-power analog
broadcasts in 2009. The UK finished in 2012.

47
00:04:04,997 --> 00:04:05,726
And with it,

48
00:04:05,726 --> 00:04:11,026
this little window to the beginning of
time quietly disappeared from our homes.

49
00:04:17,075 --> 00:04:19,585
The oldest light in the universe.

50
00:04:20,919 --> 00:04:23,500
And we used to just change the channel.

